A||||6||conductor||Freezing||||1||
Z||000000||conductor||12-14-2001||07:45 PM||||Ok, its freezing a few degrees celcius here,about 8 or so and this gives me a very annoying(and hopefully not fatal) problem.<BR>I wanted to go to a party this evening, turned the ignition on, wanted to put it in 1st gear and I couldnt!<BR>Actualy I could get it in any gear. It moved kinda freely in neutral, but that was all.<BR>Does anyone have whats going on??<BR>Last winter it happened to me as well but I had the time to warm the engine up then and now I didnt.<BR>Any ideas? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>Grtx Richard.||||reg||1
Z||000001||conductor||12-14-2001||08:36 PM||||Sorry, mistyped, I couldnt get it in any gear.<BR>I could only move it from left to right in neutral. Anyone had this problem as well?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pyric||12-14-2001||08:41 PM||||I don't think its fatal, the car I drive to work did the same thing once.  But like you I had to let it warm up.   I didn't ever find out what it was, but it hasn't happened since.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ejayerik||12-14-2001||08:42 PM||||Frozen trans or clutch cable. Pull it out, make sure there is no water in it, and lube it up with some Lithium grease. I read one that an easy way to get the grease in was to take out the cable and put the grease tube one one end, and a bag with a vacum cleaner on the other and let it pull it through. Worked great for me. The previous owner thought he was having trans problems when he sold me the car. I lubed the cable, and it shifts great now in any weather. It doesn't get freezing here but it still happens.<BR> Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-14-2001||11:11 PM||||before you fart with the cables... disconnect them at the tranny and see if the arms on the tranny are free. This is also a sign of using the wrong oil in the tranny.<P>Its way too common that people put GL5 oil into a manual tranny. This will make nearly all car manual transmissions very stiff when cold. Car trans missions usually want a GL4 group oil, ATF, or even a motor oil. You can read the stuff I have about the Muncie 5 speed in my cave...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||conductor||12-15-2001||03:05 PM||||Ok, today it shifted again and I took it for a short drive to the grocery s. When I came back, I had a hard time getting it started.<BR>I replaced the fuelfilter yesterday but it ran like crap.<BR>I had accel 8.8 and new plugs installed in spring and it only ran a few hundred miles since then, but it misses and stumbles. Start to hate that stupid engine :-(||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jefrysuko||12-15-2001||03:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B>Frozen trans or clutch cable.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The clutch on a Fiero is not cable actuated!  It is hydraulic.<P>My bet is that your tranny is fine but something is causing you clutch to not disengage fully which would make it hard to get in gear with the motor running.  Go out to the car when is cold out again and do the following.  With the motor off see if you can shift into any gear.  Then start the car and try again.  If you can shift without the car running but can't while it is then your clutch isn't disengaging fully.  If this is the case then keep reading if not, never mind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It is possible that your fluid needs to be flushed.  To much moisture could cause problems that would only show up when it is cold.  You might want to also check that you have a steal clutch pedal.  Extra force is required to depress the pedal in cold weather so it is possible that your pedal is only bending enough to cause problems when it is cold out.||||reg||1
